# Audio fingerprinting, and the connection type on Android.
#
# The audio fingerprint is one of the four or five signals every fingerprinting
# library collects, CreepJS included, and the recipe is always the same: build
# an OfflineAudioContext, run an oscillator through a DynamicsCompressor,
# render, then sum or hash the samples that come back. The result depends on
# the platform's float rounding and the compressor's implementation, so it is
# stable per device and per build, it survives clearing everything, and it is
# identical in incognito. The AnalyserNode getters are the same signal read a
# different way.
#
# One multiplier, drawn once per renderer process
# -----------------------------------------------
# Every path a page can read audio back through is scaled by a single fixed
# factor of about a hundredth of a percent. Three properties follow from
# "fixed", and each of them is why it is fixed rather than drawn per sample the
# way Cromite's version does it.
#
# It survives averaging. Independent per-sample noise does not: render the same
# graph a hundred times, take the mean, and the true value comes back. A
# constant factor is still there after any number of repetitions.
#
# It costs one random draw for the life of the process. The callers are
# per-sample loops that a visualiser runs on every frame; two RNG calls per
# sample there is a real cost in a browser whose whole pitch is efficiency.
#
# It is per process, so with site isolation it is per site - one site reads one
# consistent answer however often it asks, two sites read different answers,
# and the answer is new the next time the process is. This is the same
# reasoning as aerium-timezone.patch, and for the same reason: consistency
# within a site, variance between sites. Per document would vary more, and is
# what the existing bromite client-rects noise does, but RealtimeAnalyser has
# no document to hand, and an analyser that disagreed with an offline render on
# the same page would be a signal of its own.
#
# Nothing here touches playback. The four analyser getters produce
# visualisation data, and the offline path is a rendered result whose gain
# moves by a part in ten thousand. Decoded audio going to the speakers is not
# in any of these paths.
#
# On by default, with a chrome://flags entry to turn it off, because unlike the
# time zone override there is nothing for a user to weigh: no site behaves
# differently and nothing sounds different.
#
# baseLatency
# -----------
# Quantised to 1ms, the same way outputLatency() immediately below it already
# is. Upstream mitigated one of that pair and not the other, and there is
# nothing in the code that explains the asymmetry - base_latency_ is the audio
# hardware's buffer size over its sample rate, so it names the device as
# squarely as the measured latency does, and it is a raw double today. It
# reuses Chromium's own kOutputLatencyMaxPrecisionFactor rather than inventing
# a constant. 1ms and not the 8ms outputLatency uses without microphone
# permission: base latency is single-digit milliseconds, and 8ms would round
# most of it to zero, which would take away a value pages also schedule
# against.
#
# navigator.connection
# --------------------
# NetInfoConstantType is turned on, and that is the whole of it. The rest of
# navigator.connection is deliberately left alone, which is worth writing down
# because Cromite does rewrite it and this does not.
#
# Chromium has already mitigated most of that interface since Cromite's patch
# was written. RoundRtt() and RoundMbps() quantise rtt and downlink to 50ms and
# 25kbps buckets, cap them, and multiply by a per-host random factor first - so
# two sites already read different values for the same connection, which is the
# property we would be trying to add. saveData reflects a user setting that is
# off. What is left is effectiveType, four values that track the network rather
# than the device, and pinning it would mean a phone on a bad connection is
# told to fetch the high quality asset. That is a real cost for a two-bit
# signal that changes on its own.
#
# NetInfoConstantType is different: Chromium wrote it, wired it into
# NetworkInformation::type() and downlinkMax(), and left it off. Where those
# properties are exposed at all - Android and ChromeOS, per NetInfoDownlinkMax
# in the same file - a page can read whether the device is on wifi or cellular
# and which generation of cellular. That is about the device and where it is,
# not about the network's mood, and nothing else in this browser covers it. On
# desktop the properties are not exposed, so this changes nothing there and is
# included for symmetry with the Android build and for the day upstream flips
# NetInfoDownlinkMax on.
#
# Cromite's approach was also rejected on its mechanism, not only its scope: it
# seeds an override into the NetworkStateNotifier constructor, which is the
# object the whole renderer reads. That would move the Save-Data header and
# resource loading priorities as well as the JavaScript API, which is a much
# larger change than the one being asked for.
